@end menu

@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@code{datetime} displays the current date and time in the given @code{FORMAT}, or sets the system clock with @code{-s} or a @code{MMDDhhmm} argument. Without options it prints local time as @code{YYYYMMDDhhmmss}.
@code{timestamp} is the same binary: when compiled with @code{-DTIMESTAMP} or invoked via @code{--timestamp} it prints UTC as @code{YYYYMMDDhhmmssZ}.

The program is written in strict @code{C99} (@code{gcc -std=c99}, only @code{libc}) and follows @file{gnu_c_stileguide.md}.

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@example
datetime [OPTION]... [+FORMAT]
datetime [OPTION]... [MMDDhhmm[[CC]YY][.ss]]
timestamp [OPTION]...
@end example

@node Options
@chapter Options

@table @code
@item -d STRING
@itemx --date=STRING
Display time described by @var{STRING}, not `now'. @var{STRING} may be @code{@@EPOCH[.NANO]}, @code{YYYY-MM-DD}, @code{now}, @code{today}, @code{yesterday}, @code{tomorrow}, any string understood by @code{date -d}, or @code{TZ="Zone" STRING}.

@item --debug
Annotate parsed date and warn on standard error.

@item -f DATEFILE
@itemx --file=DATEFILE
Like @code{--date} once for each line of @var{DATEFILE}. If @var{DATEFILE} is @code{-}, read stdin.

@item -I[FMT]
@itemx --iso-8601[=FMT]
ISO 8601. @var{FMT}=@code{date} (default), @code{hours}, @code{minutes}, @code{seconds}, @code{ns}.

@item --resolution
Output resolution @code{0.000000001}.

@item -R
@itemx --rfc-email
RFC 5322. Example @code{Mon, 14 Aug 2006 02:34:56 +0000}.

@item --rfc-3339=FMT
RFC 3339. @var{FMT}=@code{date}, @code{seconds}, @code{ns}.

@item -r FILE
@itemx --reference=FILE
Display last modification time of @var{FILE}.

@item -s STRING
@itemx --set=STRING
Set time described by @var{STRING} (requires root).

@item -u
@itemx --utc
@itemx --universal
Print or set UTC.

@item --help
Display help and exit.

@item --version
Output version.

@item --timestamp
Print UTC compact seconds @code{YYYYMMDDhhmmssZ}.

@end table

Legacy formats kept: @code{-hr}/@code{--human-readable}, @code{-c}/@code{--compact}/@code{iso-basic}, @code{-cd}/@code{--calendar-date}, @code{-cdb}, @code{-od}, @code{-odb}, @code{-wd}, @code{-wdb}.

@node Format
@chapter Format

@code{FORMAT} controls output. Sequences are:

@example
%%  literal %    %a  abbrev weekday    %A  full weekday
%b  abbrev month %B  full month        %c  locale datetime
%C  century      %d  day 01            %D  %m/%d/%y
%e  space-pad    %F  %Y-%m-%d          %g/%G ISO week year
%H  hour 00-23   %I  01-12             %j  day of year
%N  nanoseconds  %q  quarter 1-4       %s  seconds since Epoch
%z  +hhmm        %:z +hh:mm           %::z +hh:mm:ss
%:::z minimal    %Z  alphabetic TZ
@end example

Flags after @code{%}: @code{-} no pad, @code{_} space, @code{0} zero, @code{+} plus, @code{^} upper, @code{#} opposite. Then width and modifier @code{E}/@code{O}.

@node Timestamp
@chapter Timestamp and Microversion

@code{timestamp} prints UTC @code{YYYYMMDDhhmmssZ} (14 digits + @code{Z}). Full version is @code{MAJOR.MINOR.TIMESTAMP}, e.g. @code{2.0.20260909083730Z}. The @code{TIMESTAMP} is regenerated each @code{make} via @file{version.h} from @code{./timestamp || ~/sbin/timestamp || date -u}. It is sortable, filename-safe, and unambiguous (@code{Z}=UTC).

@example
$ timestamp
20260909083730Z
$ TS=$(timestamp); echo "2.0.$TS"
2.0.20260909083730Z
@end example

@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@example
$ datetime                         # 20260909103730
$ datetime -hr                     # 2026-09-09 10:37:30
$ datetime -d "@0" -u +"%F %T"     # 1970-01-01 00:00:00
$ datetime --debug -d "2020-01-02"
$ datetime -Iseconds               # 2026-09-09T10:37:30+02:00
$ datetime -R                      # Wed, 09 Sep 2026 10:37:30 +0200
$ datetime -r Makefile +"%F"
$ datetime -f dates.txt +"%F"
$ datetime --resolution            # 0.000000001
$ datetime -u +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z"
$ timestamp                        # 20260909083730Z
@end example

@node GNU Compliance
@chapter GNU Compliance

This codebase follows @file{gnu_c_stileguide.md}:

@itemize
@item @code{SHELL = /bin/sh}, standard @code{prefix}/@code{bindir}/@code{mandir}, @code{INSTALL_PROGRAM} (@pxref{Makefile Conventions})
@item Function braces in column zero, name in column zero
@item Dynamic allocation (no arbitrary 4096 limits) via @code{malloc}
@item @code{getopt_long} for CLI (@pxref{Standards for Command Line Interfaces})
@item No @code{argv[0]} dispatch (use @code{--timestamp} or @code{-DTIMESTAMP})
@item Texinfo @file{manual.texi}, @file{ChangeLog}, @file{NEWS}, man page @file{man/man1/datetime.1}
@end itemize

